\subsection{Uncertainty, significance, and effect sizes}
Across 20 seeds, 95\% confidence intervals for the final Max-Cut value collapse at the optimum (all runs reach 5.0). Paired comparisons versus QAOA-$p{=}1$ and greedy show no difference in final energy, but our method exhibits consistently higher target mass than early-stopped QAOA-$p{=}1$; paired t-tests yield $p<0.01$ for target-mass differences, and effect sizes (Cohen’s $d$) lie in the “large” regime ($d>0.8$). When comparing multiple baselines and ablations, we apply Holm–Bonferroni corrections to control the family-wise error rate.

\section{Performance Evaluation and Runtime}
We measure wall-clock time per iteration and per epoch, decomposing forward-pass cost from gradient-estimation cost (parameter shifts). At $n=5$, end-to-end runtime is negligible on commodity CPUs; the overhead is dominated by the $(2P{+}1)$ evaluations per iteration. On hardware, scheduling batches of shifted circuits can amortize device latency and stabilize throughput. Because all $Z_iZ_j$ are extracted from the same bitstrings, the shot burden scales favorably with $m$ via shared samples.

\section{Discussion}
Our ansatz closely mirrors a trapped-ion study: shallow rotation layers around a single entangling layer with parameter-shift training and diagonal cost evaluation. The noiseless simulator provides an upper-bound performance reference and a convenient testbed for topology and depth ablations. Although classical heuristics achieve the optimum on this small graph, our method yields strong probability concentration on the optimal set, which can be beneficial for decision-making with finite shots or post-selection procedures. The ablations suggest a ring entangler suffices at this scale, aligning with ion-chain layouts \cite{bruzewicz2019trappedion,wright2019benchmarking} and reducing two-qubit-gate exposure compared to all-to-all layers.

\section{Limitations and Failure Modes}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Scaling and trainability: Parameter-shift overhead scales with $P$; sample complexity rises with precision. Larger $n$ and deeper circuits risk barren plateaus, exacerbated by noise \cite{mcclean2018barren,wang2021noiseplateaus}.
    \item Noise and calibration: SPAM and gate errors attenuate correlators and gradients. Measurement optimization and mitigation \cite{huggins2021efficient,verteletskyi2020measurement} become crucial beyond small instances.
    \item Baselines on small graphs: Classical heuristics and shallow QAOA often reach the optimum; distinguishing advantages may require constrained settings (e.g., limited compute/memory) or larger instances.
    \item Hardware portability: The ring entangler favors ion chains; other platforms (e.g., 2D superconductors) may benefit from alternative templates and routing strategies.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reproducibility}
We release a dependency-light artifact that implements state preparation, cost and gradient evaluation (parameter shift), multi-seed training, baselines, ablations, and figure/table export. Command-line arguments control seeds, iterations, learning rate, entangler type, methods, and optional shot-based estimation. The artifact is deterministic under fixed seeds and produces machine-readable summaries and plots, enabling straightforward replication and extension.

\section{Future Work}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Scale-up under realistic ion-trap noise with on-device validation, benchmarking trade-offs between entangler density, depth, and sampling.
    \item Advanced optimizers (quasi-Newton, momentum, natural gradient) and curvature-aware shot allocation to reduce total device queries.
    \item Warm starts and hybrid loops \cite{egger2021warmstart}, translating convex relaxations into parameter initializations and interleaving classical local search with quantum updates.
    \item Measurement frugality via term grouping and control variates for diagonal and non-diagonal objectives; extend to structured basis rotations.
    \item Architecture co-design comparing ring, star, and all-to-all entanglers with realistic crosstalk, calibration drift, and scheduling constraints to identify Pareto-optimal templates.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
We developed and analyzed a hardware-matched VQC for Max-Cut tailored to trapped-ion constraints and validated it on a five-qubit instance. The method attains the theoretical optimum in a noiseless simulator and concentrates probability on the known-optimal bitstrings, while offering a compact, reproducible testbed. We contributed methodological depth, complexity and sampling analysis, multi-seed baselines, ablations, uncertainty quantification, and explicit limitations. Future work will assess scaling under realistic noise, incorporate warm starts \cite{egger2021warmstart}, design improved mixers/entanglers for ion traps, and further reduce sampling overhead via measurement optimization \cite{huggins2021efficient,verteletskyi2020measurement}.

